--- Creates an image from an SF Symbol. Returns `nil` if no symbol of that name exists, so a symbol added in a later version of iOS simply does not draw.
--- 
--- A monochrome symbol is rendered **white**, so it can be recoloured at draw time with `tint()`. Pass a colour to bake one in instead. Symbols drawn with `image.symbol.multicolor`, `image.symbol.hierarchical` or `image.symbol.palette` carry their own colours, so draw those with `tint(255)`, since `tint()` multiplies.
--- 
--- Options may be given in any order after the name. A flag is passed as-is (`image.symbol.multicolor`, `image.symbol.weight.bold`), while an option that carries values is called with them (`image.symbol.variable(0.5)`). A bare number is the point size, and a colour tints a monochrome symbol.
--- 
--- In the code editor, tap the preview beside a symbol name to browse every symbol and pick a new one. Symbol names are provided by the operating system, and Apple adds new symbols with each release. Browse the available names, and the version each was introduced in, with Apple's SF Symbols app.
---@param name any string, the SF Symbol name, e.g. `"globe"` or `"cloud.sun.fill"`
---@param ... any 
---@return any [object Object]
